  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 AM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThe 4-Corners upper high weakens and shifts into the southeast \nCONUS, though we still remain under a broad west/northwest flow \nregime. A plume of mid level moisture lifting out of the \nintermountain west crosses South Dakota during the day. You can see \nthis broad area of mid level clouds over Nevada/Utah Idaho/Wyoming. \nThis moisture, 600mb and above, also has a very dry subcloud \nlayer resulting inverted V type soundings. Profiles also have \nwarm air aloft and while 700mb temperatures are only +8 to +11C \nwhich doesn't jump out as a strong CAP, 750mb temperature are \ncloser to +13 to +15C so NAM profiles indicate it will be \ndifficult for surface based convection but elevated convection \nis depicted in CAMS for the evening/overnight hours. HREF MUCAPE\nis only about 1500j/kg. Given the profiles, winds are the main \nconcern with any convection. Given how dry its been, dry \nlighting and fires also are a concern. \n\nThere is another plume of mid level moisture for mainly Thursday \nnight into early Friday as well. Profiles are close to moist \nadiabatic thanks to cooler mid level temperatures with easterly \nstable low level flow with the surface high just across the border \nin Canada. NAM BUFKIT profiles show its unstable during the day, \nthough there is an upper ridge moving overhead with increasing \nheights at that particular time. \n\nA southwest flow regime runs through Saturday before flow aloft \nbecomes more westerly, and ultimately northwesterly for next week. \nNBM POPs have trended down this weekend and next weekend in \nresponse, especially as a surface high moves across the region \nSunday/Monday.  Otherwise temperatures with this cooler airmass will \nbe running about 5 to 10 degrees below normal Thursday through \nSunday.",
